  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through Monday)\nIssued at 1229 PM CST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nMid to upper ridging and accompanying sinking and drying motion \nstrengthens over the Mid-South and Southeast US late week, while \nlee troughing near the surface also sharpens and gives rise to \ngusty 20 to 30 mph winds from the south over the lowlands both \nThursday and Friday. Highs Thursday will remain slow to warm \nnortheast of the Pecos River as the lee troughing itself will be \nslower to sharpen than previously forecast. This will be followed \nby southerly winds gusting above 15 mph more frequently than prior\nnights for Thursday night. The more turbulent mixing will keep \nlows from falling below the 50s F for southern and eastern \nlowlands, and from falling below the mid 40s to upper 40s F range \nfor the western higher terrain into SE NM plains and northwest \nPermian Basin. However, once lee troughing does sharpen \nsufficiently by Friday afternoon, the warming effect of \ndownsloping winds west of the boundary and strong southerly winds \neast of the boundary settles back in. In response, highs warm back\ninto the mid to upper 60s F northeast of the Pecos River, and \ninto the 70s F Eddy County Plains into Stockton Plateau, with \nupper 70s to lower 80s F down to the Rio Grande. The stronger \nwinds are expected to remain below high wind criteria and rain \nchances near zero to end the week. Therefore, no hazardous weather\nfrom either wind or rainfall is expected to close the week. Lows \nFriday night bottom out at least 5F warmer than Thursday night \neverywhere, as continued gusty winds maintain boundary layer \nmixing limiting overnight cooling, in addition on top of the \npresent reductions in overnight cooling from the warmer daytime \ntemperatures.\n\nThe mid to upper storm system develops from the Pacific SW/Desert\nSW into the Four Corners and Central Great Plains by Saturday, \naccompanied by a Pacific cold front passage and wind shift to \nwesterly by the afternoon/evening. Winds still look to remain \nbelow high wind criteria even for the usual windier locations of \nthe Guadalupes/Delawares. Highs Saturday also rise into the 70s F \nfor the southern and eastern lowlands, while only rising into the \n60s F, 50s F higher elevations of the Guadalupes farther \nnorthwest. Lows falling into the 30s F and 40s F work their way \nback in from northwest Saturday night for the western higher \nterrain into SE NM plains and northern Permian Basin, while \nremaining in the 50s F elsewhere. Rain chances slowly begin to \nincrease as overrunning occurs behind the Pacific cold front. This\ntrend of increasing rain chances continues Sunday (from very low \n15%-20% Sunday afternoon) into Monday (low 20%-35% by Monday \nafternoon), while highs in CAA behind the Pacific cold front \nbecome constrained within the 50-60F range once more, apart from \n70s F for the usual warmest locations from the Presidio Valley \ninto Big Bend. At this time, we still do not see any indications \nof a heavy rain or severe risk with returning rain chances, and \nthe bigger story this coming weekend into next week will be gusty \nwesterly winds along with a return to widespread 50s to 60s F \nhighs and upper 30s to upper 40s F lows north of the Rio Grande \nbasin.",
